    'Run a scan to surface the checks that need a human eye before they can be called a pass or a fail.' => 'Run a scan to surface the checks that need a human eye before they can be called a pass or a fail.',
    'Everything marked "not an issue", across the whole site. The per-page report can undo a single one, but only on the page it was made; this is where you check what has been waved through, or take a batch back after a check turns out to have been over-firing.' => 'Everything marked "not an issue", across the whole site. The per-page report can undo a single one, but only on the page it was made; this is where you check what has been waved through, or take a batch back after a check turns out to have been over-firing.',
    'Accessibility statement preview' => 'Accessibility statement preview',
    'Preview. This is the markup your site publishes; the styling here is the preview\'s own, and your own templates will style it differently.' => 'Preview. This is the markup your site publishes; the styling here is the preview\'s own, and your own templates will style it differently.',
    'No asset scan has run yet' => 'No asset scan has run yet',
    'Run “Scan all assets” to check every image for alt text. It runs in the background as your queue processes.' => 'Run “Scan all assets” to check every image for alt text. It runs in the background as your queue processes.',
    'No images with alt-text issues match “{search}”.' => 'No images with alt-text issues match “{search}”.',
    'Pro edition' => 'Pro edition',
    'Sent with every scanner request against the site: the PHP scan fetch, the readability fetch, and the headless Chrome browser pass. By default the scanner already identifies itself with a <code>CraftAccessibilityAudit</code> token on all three, so a single WAF or firewall rule can allow-list it. Set this only to send your own identity instead. You can reference an environment variable: type $ to see suggestions.' => 'Sent with every scanner request against the site: the PHP scan fetch, the readability fetch, and the headless Chrome browser pass. By default the scanner already identifies itself with a <code>CraftAccessibilityAudit</code> token on all three, so a single WAF or firewall rule can allow-list it. Set this only to send your own identity instead. You can reference an environment variable: type $ to see suggestions.',
    'On' => 'On',
